I wrote `src/fom_solver.c` and `make verify` prints `VERIFY OK`. The worst max-abs error over the 8 samples is 5.6e-15, against a tolerance of 1e-6. The code has no dynamic memory and no `/` operator. It reads every coefficient from `fom_data` and does every matrix product through the provided `fom_ops` routines.

What the solver does:
- **Setup and parameter updates:** both reset the state from `FOM_STATE_INIT`. The parameter term `su_p_p0 @ p0` doesn't change between iterations, so it's computed once at that point and stored in `work->acc`.
- **Each iteration:** it calls `fom_step_t0` and then `fom_step_t1`, with the schedule value `fom_sched_xi(it)` read once at the top of the iteration. The state update copies `t0` and `t1` straight into rows 0..58 of the new state, so only rows 59..73 are computed (via `fom_step_su__rest`). To skip the copy, `t0` and `t1` are written directly into those rows of `new_state`. The state and new-state buffers are swapped by pointer instead of copied.
- **Termination:** settings give a tolerance of 0, so the loop always runs `max_iter` iterations. The residual is the max-abs change over the last iteration.

Speed (`make bench`, mean time per solve):

| Version | Mean per solve |
|---|---|
| Plain `fom_iterate` | 62 µs |
| Copy selection rows, compute the rest | 44 µs |
| Write `t0`/`t1` in place into the new state | 43.8 µs |

The last two are within run-to-run noise, so the in-place step adds little on top of skipping the copied rows.

If you call `fom_solve` twice without `fom_update_params` in between, the second solve continues from the last state instead of restarting. That follows from the reset living in setup and parameter updates, as the task asked.
